{"name":"Vampire Bite","hex":"#c40233","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vampire","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mythological creature (usually humanoid and undead) said to feed on the blood or life energy of the living. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A person with habits traditionally ascribed to (literal) vampires, such as heliophobia, being a night owl, having pale skin, and so on."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To drain of energy or resources."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vampire"},{"term":"bite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cut into something by clamping the teeth.","To hold something by clamping one's teeth."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of biting.","The wound left behind after having been bitten."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vampire","blocks":[{"text":"A vampire is a mythical creature with a human-like appearance that subsists by feeding on the vital essence of humans. In European folklore, vampires are undead humanoid creatures that visit loved ones and cause mischief or deaths in the neighbourhoods they inhabited while they were alive. They wear shrouds and are often described as bloated and of ruddy or dark countenance, contrasting with the modern gaunt, pale vampire which dates from the early 19th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Philip_Burne-Jones_-_The_Vampire.jpg/330px-Philip_Burne-Jones_-_The_Vampire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vampire","wikidata":"Q46721"},{"title":"Biting","blocks":[{"text":"Biting is an action involving a set of teeth closing down on an object. It is a common zoological behavior, being found in toothed animals such as mammals, reptiles, amphibians, fishes, and arthropods. Biting is also an action humans participate in, most commonly when chewing food. Myocytic contraction of the muscles of mastication is responsible for generating the force that initiates the preparatory jaw abduction (opening), then rapidly adducts (closes) the jaw and moves the top and bottom teeth towards each other, resulting in the forceful action of a bite. Biting is one of the main functions in the lives of larger organisms, providing them the ability to forage, hunt, eat, build, play, fight, protect, and much more. Biting may be a form of physical aggression due to predatory or territorial intentions. In animals, biting can also be a normal activity, being used for eating, scratching, carrying objects, preparing food for young, removing ectoparasites or irritating foreign objects, and social grooming. Humans can have the tendency to bite each other whether they are children or adults."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Lion_Biting_Brother%27s_Tail_%2821277465378%29.jpg/330px-Lion_Biting_Brother%27s_Tail_%2821277465378%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biting","wikidata":"Q4918888"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vampire (1895) by Edvard Munch","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEdvard_Munch_-_Vampire_(1895)_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/72/Ernst6-thumb.gif/250px-Ernst6-thumb.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/72/Ernst6-thumb.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/72/Ernst6-thumb.gif/250px-Ernst6-thumb.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of a vampire from Max Ernst's Une Semaine de Bonté (1934)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AErnst6-thumb.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/GarlicBasket.jpg/500px-GarlicBasket.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/GarlicBasket.jpg/1280px-GarlicBasket.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/GarlicBasket.jpg/500px-GarlicBasket.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGarlicBasket.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Thebible33.jpg/500px-Thebible33.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Thebible33.jpg/500px-Thebible33.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThebible33.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Salzburg_Kajetanerkirche_Weihwasserbecken.jpg/500px-Salzburg_Kajetanerkirche_Weihwasserbecken.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia